🖥️ Lab 1: Understanding Network Interfaces

Step 1: Open Wireshark as Administrator.

Step 2: Open Command Prompt (cmd) and run ipconfig.

Step 3: Note the adapter with an active IPv4 Address.

Step 4: In Wireshark, match that adapter with the active sparkline graph (e.g., Wi-Fi or Ethernet).






📡 Lab 2: Your First Packet Capture

Step 1: Double-click your active interface in Wireshark to start capturing.

Step 2: Open cmd and run ping 8.8.8.8 -t. Let it run for 10 seconds.

Step 3: Press Ctrl + C in cmd to stop the ping.

Step 4: Click the Red Square 🟥 in Wireshark to stop the capture.






🔍 Lab 3: Filtering the Noise (ICMP)

Step 1: Type icmp in the display filter bar and press Enter.

Step 2: Click on an Echo (ping) request packet.

Step 3: In the middle pane, expand Internet Protocol Version 4 to see Source/Destination IPs.

Step 4: Expand Internet Control Message Protocol to see Type 8 (Request) or Type 0 (Reply).







🦴 Lab 4: Understanding Packet Anatomy

Step 1: Select any packet from the list.

Step 2: Pane 1 (Top): Review the summary (Time, Source, Destination, Protocol, Info).

Step 3: Pane 2 (Middle): Review the OSI layers (Frame, Ethernet II, IPv4, Protocol). Expand Ethernet II to find MAC Addresses.

Step 4: Pane 3 (Bottom): Review the raw Hex/ASCII data.







🌐 Lab 5: DNS Investigation

Step 1: Click the Blue Shark Fin 🦈 to start a new capture (select Continue without Saving).

Step 2: In cmd, run nslookup google.com and nslookup github.com.

Step 3: Stop the capture 🟥.

Step 4: Apply filter: dns.

Step 5: Select a Standard query packet. Expand Domain Name System (query) -> Queries to see the requested domain.

Step 6: Select a Standard query response packet. Expand Domain Name System (response) -> Answers to see the returned IP address.






🕸️ Lab 6: Website Traffic (HTTP)

Step 1: Start a new capture 🦈.

Step 2: Open a browser and visit http://neverssl.com.

Step 3: Stop the capture 🟥.

Step 4: Apply filter: http.

Step 5: Select the GET / HTTP/1.1 packet.

Step 6: Expand Hypertext Transfer Protocol in the middle pane to view cleartext data like Host and User-Agent.







🤝 Lab 7: TCP 3-Way Handshake

Step 1: Using the capture from Lab 6, clear the filter bar.

Step 2: Apply filter: tcp and ip.addr == [IP_of_neverssl.com].

Step 3: Locate the first 3 packets of the connection at the top.

Step 4: Observe the Info column for the handshake sequence: SYN ➔ SYN, ACK ➔ ACK.









🕵️‍♂️ Lab 8: Secret Chat Server

Step 1: Open cmd (Terminal 1) and start a server: ncat -lvp 4444.

Step 2: Start a new Wireshark capture 🦈 on "Adapter for loopback traffic capture".

Step 3: Open a second cmd (Terminal 2) and connect: ncat 127.0.0.1 4444.

Step 4: Type messages in Terminal 2 (e.g., Hello Server, Secret Data) and hit Enter.

Step 5: Stop the capture 🟥.

Step 6: Apply filter: tcp.port == 4444.

Step 7: Right-click any packet with a PSH, ACK flag ➔ select Follow ➔ TCP Stream to read the intercepted chat.
